TI  - Process Mining Perspectives in Software Engineering: A Systematic Literature Review
AU  - Jaqueline Urrea-Contreras, Silvia
AU  - Flores-Rios, Brenda L.
AU  - Angélica Astorga-Vargas, María
AU  - Ibarra-Esquer, Jorge E.
PY  - 2021
AB  - In the last decade, Process Mining has established as an emerging discipline for being oriented to the discovery, monitoring, and improvement of processes. This article presents the results of a Systematic Literature Review that reveals the state of the art of Process Mining perspectives in Software Engineering from 2010 to 2020, with the purpose of adequately characterizing software processes that are mostly complex. Twelve primary studies were selected and classified according to their type of publication, conceptual definition of Process Mining perspectives, their implementation in case studies and the successful contributions to the challenges and opportunities of implementing the perspectives identified in Software Engineering. The results identify four Process Mining perspectives (control- flow, organizational, case, and time), highlighting the use of the control-flow perspective in the cases of discovery of process models (flows and order of execution activities) and the organization- al perspective in the identification of human resource interaction. Although Process Mining perspectives have been applied in the Software Engineering domain, the lack of a standardized definition for the term Process Mining perspective and, in turn, for each of them, is still highlighted.
