TY  - CPAPER
TI  - State-Aware Age of Information: Evaluating Content-Relevant Freshness in Wireless Systems
AU  - Wang, Dazhi
AU  - Zhou, Siyuan
PY  - 2025
AB  - In cyber-physical systems such as digital twins and industrial IoT, maintaining timely and informative state updates is essential for reliable system operation and control. The Age of Information (AoI) has been widely used to quantify data freshness, yet it treats all updates equally without considering whether they reflect meaningful state changes. As a result, AoI may overstate freshness in systems where source dynamics evolve slowly or insignificantly. To overcome this limitation, we propose a novel metric, State-Aware Age of Information (SA-AoI), which extends the classical AoI framework by incorporating state variation into freshness evaluation. SA-AoI selectively considers only those updates that capture actual state changes, and further penalizes updates based on the degree of difference between the source and received states. This enables a more accurate measure of both temporal freshness and semantic relevance. We model the state evolution using an N-state discrete-time Markov chain (DTMC), and analyze SA-AoI under a G/Geo/1 queuing model with a preemptive Last-Come-First-Served (LCFS) discipline. We derive a closed-form expression for the average SA-AoI and validate our analysis through Monte Carlo simulations. The results offer new perspectives for designing efficient, state-aware update strategies in wireless communication systems.
