TI  - Feature Engineering-Driven Evolution Analysis and Detection of Unknown Attacks
AU  - Li, Gang
AU  - Liu, Sunjun
AU  - Ao, Menghao
AU  - Zhang, Xiao
PY  - 2025
AB  - The growing openness and advancement of cyber attack technologies enhance attackers' ability to exploit existing attacks/vulnerabilities, driving the constant emergence of unknown cyber attacks. Timely detection of such attacks is crucial for mitigating loss risks to organizations and information infrastructures. This paper proposes Feature Engineering-Driven Evolution Analysis and Detection of Unknown Attacks. Specifically, we first generate isolation trees via Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) and Monte Carlo method using known attacks' feature distribution, then calculate path length threshold (utilizing unknown attacks' shorter paths in trees) and centroid deviation threshold for dualthreshold identification. Next, we define known sample features as “genes”, generate new features via multiple evolution strategies, and label them (known variants/unknown samples) through multi-isolation-tree voting. Finally, we iteratively train a Random Forest with evolved features and select the optimal model as the final detector. Extensive experiments on real datasets show: $\mathbf7 8. 4 3 %$ precision, $\mathbf9 9. 6 0 %$ recall, $\mathbf8 7. 7 5 %$ F1-score for samecategory unknown attacks; 87.71 % precision, 99.90 % recall, 93.41 % F1-score for different-category ones. Our method also outperforms VAE-and GAN-based sample generation methods in unknown attack detection.
