AB  - Sequential recommendation systems often use IDbased embeddings, which are efficient but lack semantic richness and generalization. Large language models (LLMs) can capture contextual information, yet their use in recommendation is limited. We propose a model that initializes recommendation sequences with token-based LLM representations, transferring linguistic knowledge into item representations. Unlike traditional embeddings, our method applies subword and contextual encoding to preserve semantic detail across diverse items. On benchmarks like Amazon-Books and MovieLens-1M, our approach achieves higher accuracy with less memory and training time than SASRec, BERT4Rec, and GPT-based recommenders. Ablation studies further show faster convergence and reduced overfitting. These results demonstrate that LLM token-based initialization is an efficient and cost-effective paradigm for advancing sequential recommendation.
