AB  - This paper presents a robust one-degree-offreedom (1-DoF) Sigmoid-based Proportional-IntegralDerivative (SPID) controller optimized using the Nonlinear Sine Cosine Algorithm (NSCA) for an Automatic Voltage Regulator (AVR) system. Conventional PID controllers often struggle with parameter variations and external disturbances, affecting the AVR stability and performance. The proposed SPID controller addresses these challenges by enhancing the system’s robustness and dynamic response through nonlinear sigmoid functions. The NSCA efficiently fine-tunes the controller parameters, balancing exploration and exploitation in the optimization process. Simulation results demonstrate that the SPID controller outperforms conventional PID and other metaheuristic-tuned controllers, achieving up to 59.57% faster settling time, 100% reduction in overshoot, and 69.41% lower steady-state error. This approach significantly improves the voltage regulation in power systems, enhancing stability and reliability under varying load conditions, and offers a robust solution for real world power system applications
