TY  - JOUR
TI  - Drought and life‐history strategies in <i>Heliophila</i> (Brassicaceae)
AU  - Ladislav Mucina
AU  - Klaus Mummenhoff
AU  - P.J.D. Winter
AU  - Adriaan van Niekerk
AU  - Terezie Mandáková
AU  - Martin A. Lysák
PY  - 2023
AB  - Monroe et al. (2019) claim that drought regimes predict life-history strategies in plants, using the taxonomically complex southern African genus Heliophila (Brassicaceae) as a model. The authors focused on a relatively short period (34 yr) of remote-sensing data, focusing on the Vegetative Health Index (VHI), ostensibly to represent drought frequency, and the authors use the term ‘historical drought regimes’ as a background to gain insight into the evolution of life histories in the genus. Here, we argue that some of the procedures and conclusions presented by Monroe et al. (2019) are either simplistic or flawed due to methodological and conceptual errors. We shall demonstrate that Monroe et al. (2019): (1) relied on an inaccurate distribution data set; (2) uncritically presented and applied the phylogenetic data; and (3) presented a conceptual error by using a short-term climatic dataset to infer change in life history on evolutionary time scales. The distribution of plant species is not only a function of ecological and evolutionary processes but inference thereof also relies on the correct taxonomic identity of specimens. The inevitable taxonomic bias and misidentification in datasets can only be removed by the significant effort of critical re-identification of the vouchers involved. The main source of the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF https://www.gbif.org) Heliophila distribution data used by Monroe et al. (2019) is the South African BODATSA database, with which we are familiar. We have validated much of the theory in situ for most species of this genus and, thus, are able to readily notice where an occurrence record is falsely reported for an area, or where data were missing for important areas in the species' native range. These data are still marred by identification errors, mainly rooted in pre-existing poorly solved taxonomic issues surrounding this genus (Mummenhoff et al., 2005; Al-Shehbaz, 2019, 2020a,b), as well as difficulties with the identification of depauperate specimens. Monroe et al. (2019) did not appear to verify the link between the name and georeference of the vouchers involved. A tentative assessment of the plotted occurrence data for all the taxa (fig. 2 in Monroe et al., 2019) and per species (fig. S1 in Monroe et al., 2019) showed that only 10 of 42 species had no clear outliers which, to our knowledge, represents errors deviating from the reported distribution ranges (Marais, 1970). The only species whose ranges were correctly represented are H. amplexicaulis, H. carnosa, H. collina, H. cornellsbergia, H. crithmifolia, H. esterhuyseniae, H. gariepina, H. rigidiuscula, and H. tricuspidata. While the effect of this problem may not be significant for some widespread taxa supported by many records, it would have a significant negative effect on both the accuracy and precision of the environmental data used by Monroe et al. (2019) for more localized taxa and the taxa supported with fewer records. Likewise, a failure to critically assess the taxonomic validity of data reported in earlier studies resulted in taxon identification errors being simply carried over from one study to the next. Previous studies (Mummenhoff et al., 2005; Mandáková et al., 2012) are now known by us to contain errors and unsubstantiated assertions regarding the identification of some vouchers (H. arenaria, H. deserticola, H. minima, H. suavissima, and H. trifurca). Consequently, the assignment of samples to a named taxon is not reliable for these data – an area that is always a risk in such analyses. The uncritical acceptance of questionable taxon identities following these previous analyses thus found its way to the phylogenetic analysis of Monroe et al. (2019). This uncertainty appears to carry through to the assignment of the life-history category, which is deduced indirectly from the literature, rather than from empirical population-specific data directly associated with these vouchers. For instance, Monroe et al. (2019; Supporting Information Table S1) categorize H. ephemera as an annual, this is an unsubstantiated assumption (also expressed by Goldblatt & Manning, 2000; Mummenhoff et al., 2005). However, further inspection of the original description (Bean, 1990) and the ecological circumstances suggest that it might, in fact, be a short-lived perennial. Until more field-based observations are available, the species cannot be treated as an annual. Monroe et al. (2019) note they used the published ITS data (Mandáková et al., 2012) to construct their phylogeny. Furthermore, they stated: ‘A maximum clade credibility tree with branch lengths as relative time was estimated by summarizing data from six runs of 100 000 000 generations of Bayesian Markov chain Monte Carlo conducted in BEAST 2’. However, this statement fails to provide important information on how the rate of evolution was set (via clock.rate or ucld.mean parameters). Bayesian analysis also lacks important settings needed for repeat analysis, such as tree sampling or burn-in, and no convergence and mixing tests of runs were performed using well-established programs, such as Tracer (Rambaut et al., 2018) or AWTY (Nylander et al., 2008). As no details are provided in the Supporting Information, the Beast analysis cannot be independently verified. Although Monroe et al. (2019) reported that ‘An alignment of ITS I and II sequences for 21 annual and 21 perennial Heliophila species was obtained from the authors of Mandáková et al. (2012)’, they do not indicate which accessions and/or GenBank accession numbers were used for their phylogenetic analysis, the ITS alignment, and for the ITS tree with the outgroup species. The phylogeny presented in Monroe et al.'s fig. 1(a) is an ancestral state estimation (Annual vs Perennial) performed by Phytools (Revell, 2012) and which uses the ITS tree generated in Beast. Monroe et al. (2019), while presenting a fully resolved Heliophila phylogeny (fig. 1a), did not provide any statistical support values (posterior probabilities), which are vital to the evaluation of the reliability of nodes. Accordingly, the credibility of the reconstructed transitions from annual to perennial life history becomes questionable. The authors claim that ‘The topology of the estimated Heliophila phylogeny was consistent with previous studies (Mummenhoff et al., 2005; Mandáková et al., 2012)’. However, this is not the case, as the ITS trees in both cited articles were unresolved, with a trichotomy of three subgeneric clades (A, B, and C). The tree presented by Monroe et al. (2019) is resolved, with clade C being sister to clades A + B. Thus, Monroe et al. (2019)'s tree is consistent with the previous trees only in retrieving three subgeneric clades. Statistical support (posterior probability values, jackknife) for basal nodes in previous (Mummenhoff et al., 2005; Mandáková et al., 2012) and most recent (Dogan et al., 2021) ITS-based phylogenies were mostly weak, suggesting that the relationship between the main infrageneric clades cannot be resolved using the comparable data set, and should best be interpreted as a basal polytomy. Monroe et al. (2019) used VHI (over a period of 34 yr) as a proxy for ‘drought frequency’. However, there is more to ‘drought stress’ than its frequency. It matters if the droughts are long or short in duration, and it also matters if they are severe or light in intensity. Monroe et al. (2019) cautiously refer to ‘drought regimes’, yet they approximated only ‘drought frequency’. The assumption that the frequency of Normalised Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI; and hence VHI) depressions would be suitably informative about drought stress does not appear to represent a reliable approach. Monroe et al. (2019) found that ‘annuals rather than perennial species occur in environments where droughts are significantly more frequent’ and further suggest that ‘these results yield compelling support for longstanding theoretical predictions by revealing the importance of drought frequency and predictability to explain plant life history’. In other words, the authors posit that drought frequency is a factor driving the evolution of plant life histories. We re-analyzed the relationship between selected climatic (94) and nonclimatic (8, also called ‘alternative’) variables (mainly describing the substrate) in light of the patterns of life history of all species involved in the molecular tree (fig. 1). We used Classification & Regression Tree (CART) and Random Forest (RF) machine learning tools, a set of climatic (and nonclimatic/alternative) variables and the target classes were Annual and Perennial. The lists of variables and details of the analyses can be found in the Methods S1. Our analyses (total accuracy of the CART and RF models is 66.5 and 68.7%, respectively) revealed that the most effective variables in driving the separation between the Annual and Perennial target classes are long-term rainfall during the months of December, January, and February (Median precipitation 12, 01, and 02, respectively; see Table S1), indicating that climatic seasonality matters. We concur that regularly occurring drought stress has often been (in the form of ‘climatic seasonality’) implied as driving evolutionary processes, including speciation, extinction, and migrations (e.g. Mucina & Wardell-Johnson, 2011, and references therein). Although the data we used cover only 40–60 old records, the seasonality itself is a several million-year-old phenomenon in the Cape, rooted in Mid-Miocene and possibly as far as Oligocene climate change events (e.g. Feakins & DeMenocal, 2010). Either way, the drought regimes, as captured by a 34-yr window and the method used by Monroe et al. (2019), could not be seen even as a surrogate for climatic seasonality. Monroe et al. (2019) state that they found annual life history to be ancestral in Heliophila. This is logically flawed following inspection of their phylogenetic tree (fig. 1): the basal clade (the one including H. tricuspidata and H. glauca) is at the very least dominated by perennials. The true life-history profile (perennial) of H. ephemera (also a member of the basal clade) makes this even less likely. In this light, the repeatedly inferred transitions from Annual to Perennial and Perennial to Annual appear to be artifacts as well. This work was supported by a research grant from the Czech Science Foundation (19-07487S) and grant no. 9345-13 from the Committee for Research and Exploration of the National Geographic Society. LM acknowledges the logistic support of the Iluka Chair in Vegetation Science and Biogeography at Murdoch University. None declared. Methods S1 Classification & Regression Tree and Random Forest analyses. Table S1 Results of the Classification & Regression Tree and Random Forest analyses. Please note: Wiley is not responsible for the content or functionality of any Supporting Information supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing material) should be directed to the New Phytologist Central Office. Please note: The publisher is not responsible for the content or functionality of any supporting information supplied by the authors. Any queries (other than missing content) should be directed to the corresponding author for the article.
