TY  - CPAPER
TI  - Using Monte Carlo simulation for assessing software reliability
AU  - Carnes, Patrick
PY  - 2006
AB  - System reliability is a combination of the reliability of the Computer
Software Configuration Items (CSCIs) of the system as well as the
Hardware Configuration Items (HCIs) of the system. While hardware
reliability decreases over time, software reliability normally improves
over time. Therefore, during the later stage of the system lifetime, the
software reliability component should have an ever-decreasing adverse
impact on system reliability. However, software experiences an almost
constant maintenance cycle in which either corrective, adaptive,
perfective or enhancement activities occur. Since these activities
normally introduce defects into the code, software seldom realizes full
reliability growth and thus has more of an adverse impact on system
reliability than hardware component. Key words: software development,
software reliability, software testing.
