TY  - CPAPER
TI  - From glossy brochure to successful project usage: Guidelines to the
selection of discrete-event process simulation software
AU  - Williams, EJ
PY  - 1999
AB  - Discrete-event process simulation is well-regarded, and most properly
so, among industrial, process, and Discrete-event process simulation is
well-regarded, production engineers and their managers in industrial
manufacturing. Challenges frequently confronting these engineers and
managers involve the quick and cost-effective achievement of process
improvement, scheduling policy, manufacturing efficiency, and
productivity gains within a context of keen global competition.
Simulation, correctly and effectively applied, is a powerful assistant
to these engineers and managers.
A generation ago (and that's not too many years in the contexts of
highly competitive manufacturing and of computer software development),
the building of process simulation models was typically undertaken using
either a general-purpose or an application-specific computer language.
Now, many software tools compete for the attention of the industrial
engineer and the model builder. This paper provides guidance in the
selection and usage of such software tools.
