TY  - CPAPER
TI  - TK Aware Business Process Simulation: A Case Study With Slovenian
High-Achieving Company From the SME Sector
AU  - Majewski, Grzegorz Marek
AU  - Delak, Bostjan
AU  - Damij, Nadja
PY  - 2014
AB  - This paper is investigating the methods and approaches to tacit
knowledge (TK) aware business process simulation (BPS). The contemporary
literature review on the subject reveals that the current methods,
techniques and approaches in the BPS domain do not fully realize the
potential for improvement that is related to TK of staff members in an
organization. Tacit Knowledge is very important part of staff members'
knowledge and expertise and very often constitutes competitive advantage
for an organization. Therefore it is very important to include it in
BPS. This research is aiming at filling this gap in knowledge. It is
providing a starting point on how to enhance BPS techniques to allow TK
related consideration. It is necessary to take into account multiple
factors from both KM as well as BPS domains in order to be able
efficiently model tacit knowledge within the frames of business
processes. Authors propose an approach based on well-known software
engineering technique namely Black Box concept. This approach is put
into practice with the usage of one standard BPS technique - interviews
with staff members and further computer-aided simulation. This technique
was identified as an appropriate one for this sort of enhancement. In
the next step the BPS model (derived from a black box concept) that is
TK aware is demonstrated and discussed. This model is verified by the
means of a case study with a high achieving Slovenian company from the
Small and Medium Enterprises (SME) sector. After that results of the
interviews are presented together with the information regarding
computer-aided simulation using iGrafix software. Based on these
improvements to the underlying model are suggested. Apart from that
guidance on the methodology together other relevant considerations are
demonstrated and discussed. At the end implications for BPS and KM
practitioners are provided as well as opportunities that the TK-aware
BPS can provide for the modern organizations.
