TY  - CPAPER
TI  - Using software process simulation to assist software development
organizations in making good enough quality decisions
AU  - Collofello, JS
AU  - Rus, I
AU  - Ramakrishnan, V
AU  - Musco, C
AU  - Dooley, K
PY  - 1998
AB  - As competition intensifies in the software development marketplace,
software development organizations continue to search for ways of
shortening the development time and reducing the cost of their software
products while not sacrificing quality. The development time, cost and
quality of a product are interrelated and, unfortunately, a positive
effect on one often produces a negative impact on another. In light of
this, a new paradigm ``good enough software quality″ is being to gain
attention. A ``good enough software quality″ analysis involves a
careful consideration of the costs of defect prevention and detection
activities as well as the risks of undiscovered defects and the benefits
of reduced development time. This analysis must be performed from
customer and market perspectives including the different users of the
product, market considerations and the time frame that the product will
be used.
To assist software development organizations in making ``good enough
software quality″ decisions we have developed a system dynamics model
of the software development process.
Our model incorporates the cost and effectiveness of defect prevention
techniques (such as software inspections), defect detection techniques
(such as software testing) and the cost of failing to detect defects.
Several simulation runs are presented to illustrate the model's usage.
