TY  - JOUR
TI  - PROCESS MATURITY AS FRAMEWORK FOR CASE TOOL SELECTION
AU  - PFLEEGER, SL
PY  - 1991
AB  - The maturity of the software development process can provide guidelines
for the types of computer-aided software engineering (CASE) tools that
are appropriate for an organization to use.  The paper outlines the
characteristics of five levels of maturity, corresponding to the
Software Engineering Institute's process maturity levels.  It shows how
level 1 projects should look for tools to provide structure and control.
At level 2, tools for requirements specification, project management,
and configuration management should be introduced.  By level 3, the
project should be using tools to measure quality and complexity, to
support design and coding, and to guide testing and integration.  A
project database management system, simulation tools, reliability
models, and impact analysis are appropriate at level 4, while process
programming and process simulation tools would be helpful at level 5.
