TI  - What do Developers want? An Advisor approach for Developer Priorities
AU  - Sharma, Vibhu Saujanya
AU  - Mehra, Rohit
AU  - Kaulgud, Vikrant
PY  - 2017
AB  - On a typical work day, a software developer is swamped to know answers
to a multitude of questions to gain diverse insights into the project
environment, spanning multiple categories including code, quality and
guidance. Due to client mandates, the project environment employs a lot
of heterogeneous tools, thus making the relevant information retrieval
process fairly complex and therefore it is important to know which
insights are most important to the developer. In this paper, we present
results from a survey we conducted on a pool of 27 developers from the
development team in the delivery center, by asking them to rate a set of
25 Questions, prioritising them as most, moderate and least important to
be answered automatically during the software evolution process. We also
introduce the concept of Smart Advisor for developers, an intelligence
augmentation framework that employs domain and knowledge modelling and
in-process analytics to automatically provide important insights and
answer developer queries, using a conversational and interactive user
interface.
