TY  - JOUR
TI  - Reinforcement-Learning-Based Agile Transmission Strategy for Networked
Radar System Anti-Jamming
AU  - Zhang, Zhongxin
AU  - Yuan, Ye
AU  - Sun, Jun
AU  - Han, Kuoye
AU  - Li, Huiyong
AU  - Yi, Wei
PY  - 2025
AB  - Radars employing agile waveforms can enhance detection performance by
leveraging parameter agility under jamming environment. To further
exploit and augment the detection capabilities of networked radars, this
article introduces an agile transmission strategy. This strategy enables
radars to dynamically choose between detection and covert operation,
improving anti-jamming capabilities while maintaining robust detection
performance. To optimize the transmission configurations of radar nodes,
we propose a reinforcement-learning-based Markov decision process model.
This approach enhances pulse accumulation efficiency and anti-jamming
performance by learning the jammer's strategy through interaction. Given
the nonlinear relationship between detection probability and pulse
accumulation, we use detection probability, rather than the
signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio, as the reward function. This
choice allows for a more precise alignment with detection requirements.
To minimize training time and support real-time online application,
\$Q\$-learning is utilized to derive the optimal strategy in electronic
countermeasure environments. In the scenario considered, multiple radars
are mounted on mobile platforms and intermittently emit pulses to reduce
the probability of interception and mitigate the risk of being targeted.
Real-time anti-jamming strategies are developed during the electronic
countermeasure process. Simulation results confirm that the proposed
method effectively reduces radar jamming probability while enhancing
detection performance.
