TY  - CPAPER
TI  - Test case generation through sensitivity analysis of large scale safety
critical software for nuclear power plants
AU  - Kim, Sung Ho
AU  - Kim, Hang Bae
AU  - Seong, Poong Hyun
PY  - 2007
AB  - Large scale safety critical software for nuclear power plants with
complex calculation algorithms is widely implemented using function
block diagram (FBD) tools on the programmable logic controllers. In this
case, software engineers usually take different steps than the software
engineering procedures for general control systems by customizing the
function block library to constitute a project. To guarantee a given
level of software reliability quantitatively, several steps of tests are
performed, and test cases should be selected effectively to cover the
defects remaining in the software. This means that the strategy for the
optimal selection of test cases is very important since there can be
many input variables, and some input values may not be selected for test
even if they may be the critical branch points for trip generation. The
basic concept for selecting test inputs is general binning of the input
domain as proposed by K. W. Miller et al[1]. They proposed to divide
the input domain in many parts, and select test cases from some of the
bins. To select the optimal test case sets, however, the software
response to input changes can be considered. The operational profiles
can also be considered to estimate how frequently a software section is
executed. This can be a measure to estimate the software failure
probability considering the close relationship between input profiles
and software sensitivity. The sensitivity of the software will also tell
something about the defect-to-failure transition probability. In this
paper, the software engineering steps to customize the function block
diagram library for generating target codes are described, and the
strategies to select optimal test cases arc proposed.
